🚀 Week 6 – Day 1 Java Generics: Classes, Methods & Interfaces ☕

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Generics allow classes, methods, and interfaces to work with different data types while maintaining type safety.

👉 Introduced in Java 5 to:

✔ Eliminate type casting
✔ Improve code reusability
✔ Provide compile-time type checking

---

🎯 Why Generics?

Without Generics:

```java id="g1"
ArrayList list = new ArrayList();

list.add("Java");
list.add(100);
```

👉 Different data types can be stored, causing runtime errors.

---

✅ With Generics

```java id="g2"
ArrayList<String> list =
        new ArrayList<>();

list.add("Java");
```

✔ Only String values allowed.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. Generic Class

A generic class can work with any data type.

---

# 📌 Syntax

```java id="g3"
class ClassName<T> {
}
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="g4"
class Box<T> {

    private T value;

    void setValue(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    T getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}
```

---

▶️ Using Generic Class

```java id="g5"
Box<String> box =
        new Box<>();

box.setValue("Java");

System.out.println(
        box.getValue());
```

---

📌 Multiple Type Parameters

```java id="g6"
class Pair<K, V> {

    K key;
    V value;

    Pair(K key, V value) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
    }
}
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 2. Generic Methods

A method can have its own type parameter.

---

📌 Syntax

```java id="g7"
<T> returnType methodName(T value)
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="g8"
class Demo {

    static <T> void printData(T data) {
        System.out.println(data);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        printData("Java");
        printData(100);
        printData(3.14);
    }
}
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 3. Generic Interfaces

Interfaces can also be generic.

---

📌 Syntax

```java id="g9"
interface InterfaceName<T> {
}
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="g10"
interface Printer<T> {

    void print(T value);
}
```

---

📌 Implementation

```java id="g11"
class StringPrinter
        implements Printer<String> {

    public void print(String value) {
        System.out.println(value);
    }
}
```

